Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: enable and size the PPE DRAM FOE table

airoha_ppe_hw_init() programs REG_PPE_TB_HASH_CFG with the DRAM hash modes
but leaves PPE_DRAM_TABLE_EN cleared, so the PPE only searches the
8192-entry SRAM table while the 16384-entry DRAM table allocated next to it
is never used. With 8192 effective slots a PCDN workload of more than 8k
concurrent sessions saturates the table and keeps churning the slow path.

Set PPE_DRAM_TABLE_EN so the full 24576-entry table is searched, and size
the software array for the hardware index space rather than for the usable
entry count: the PPE addresses the SRAM of every PPE first and then the DRAM
table, so on the single-PPE AN7583 the DRAM window starts at index 0x4000
and leaves an 8K hole. An array sized for 24576 entries lets the hardware
write at indices 0x6000-0x7fff past the end of the buffer.

Zero the whole DRAM part of that buffer during the initial flush so stale
data can never be interpreted as a valid binding.

Verified on the Nokia XG-040G-MF: ppe/foe reads up to 0x7fff, flows hashed
into 0x6000-0x7fff resolve to the matching A[hash] entry, and every reason
row in ppe/diag reports nohash=0 under a full PCDN load.

Signed-off-by: zhoufl <zhoufl@localhost>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.h
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.h
@@ -68,6 +68,12 @@
 #define PPE_SRAM_NUM_ENTRIES		(8 * 1024)
 #define PPE_STATS_NUM_ENTRIES		(4 * 1024)
 #define PPE_DRAM_NUM_ENTRIES		(16 * 1024)
+/* The PPE addresses its tables through one flat index space: the SRAM of
+ * every PPE first, then the DRAM table. EN7581 has two PPEs (16K entries of
+ * SRAM) so the DRAM table starts at index 0x4000, which leaves an unused 8K
+ * hole on the single-PPE AN7583.
+ */
+#define PPE_DRAM_IDX_BASE		0x4000
 #define PPE_ENTRY_SIZE			80
 #define PPE_RAM_NUM_ENTRIES_SHIFT(_n)	(__ffs((_n) >> 10))
 
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c
@@ -75,7 +75,12 @@
 {
 	u32 sram_num_entries = airoha_ppe_get_total_sram_num_entries(ppe);
 
-	return sram_num_entries + PPE_DRAM_NUM_ENTRIES;
+	/* Size the table for the hardware index space, not for the number of
+	 * usable entries: the PPE indexes the DRAM table relative to
+	 * REG_PPE_TB_BASE, so an undersized table makes it write entries past
+	 * the end of the buffer.
+	 */
+	return max(sram_num_entries, PPE_DRAM_IDX_BASE + PPE_DRAM_NUM_ENTRIES);
 }
 
 bool airoha_ppe_is_enabled(struct airoha_eth *eth, int index)
@@ -182,6 +182,7 @@
 			      FIELD_PREP(PPE_SRAM_TABLE_EN_MASK, 1) |
 			      FIELD_PREP(PPE_SRAM_HASH1_EN_MASK, 1) |
 			      FIELD_PREP(PPE_SRAM_HASH1_MODE_MASK, 1) |
+			      FIELD_PREP(PPE_DRAM_TABLE_EN_MASK, 1) |
 			      FIELD_PREP(PPE_DRAM_HASH1_MODE_MASK, 3));
 
 		airoha_fe_rmw(eth, REG_PPE_TB_CFG(i),
@@ -1613,6 +1614,15 @@
 			return err;
 	}
 
+	/* The PPE indexes the DRAM table relative to REG_PPE_TB_BASE, so the
+	 * buffer has to cover the whole hardware index space, not just the
+	 * usable entries. Zero all of it so stale data past the SRAM window
+	 * can never look like a valid binding.
+	 */
+	memset(hwe + sram_num_entries, 0,
+	       (airoha_ppe_get_total_num_entries(ppe) - sram_num_entries) *
+	       sizeof(*hwe));
+
 	return 0;
 }
 
